A||||5||Richard Parnell||Fuel pump problem||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||11-07-2000||01:48 PM||||We have finished the V8 install but have a problem.  The fuel pump does not seem to work suddenly.  This was the last thing that I thought would give a problem.  The last time I started the old 4 cyl up the pump worked perfectly.  Now you don't hear it start up when you turn on the ignition.  I seem to remember that there is a relation between the oil pressure switch and fuel pump switch.  As far as I know this is not ecm driven as we have removed the ecm from any function in the car.  Does someone have a suggestion as to what the problem is?  No wires to the fuel pump were touched and there is power to the pump.  It just does not pump. Any suggestions will be appreciated.  If we can get this solved I can take the car out for a test drive today.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-07-2000||06:50 PM||||In a stock fiero.<P>The pump is run by the ECM for a few seconds when the key is turned to run. That precharges the lines to give the engine enough gas to kick over.<P>The pump is then run by a switch on the oil pressure sender. No oil pressure no fuel. If all you have is an oil pressure switch then you have to crank the engine enough to develope oil pressure to activate the switch. This switch is in the stock OP sender. It bypasses the ECM controlled relay when it closes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||11-07-2000||08:58 PM||||Ogre is correct, the ECM runs the pump via the relay for about 2 seconds when the ignition is turned on, and also runs it while the engine is cranking. The oil pressure switch takes over when the engine builds pressure. I assume you removed the relay, as it was part of the EFI harness. You'll need to install a relay and connect it's coil to the small purple wire that goes to the starter, and wire the oil switch parallel to the relays contacts. That will get the pump running during cranking, and keep it running as long as you have oil pressure. If you need help, send me an email and I'll fax or email a simple schematic.<P>Why not use a mechanical pump?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||11-07-2000||11:43 PM||||If you dropped a V-8 in a 4 cylinder car you need to change the pump anyway, it just won't supply enough pressure to feed the 8.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Richard Parnell||11-08-2000||01:08 PM||||Thanks for the info guys.  We figured it out.  By the way. The 6 cyl fuel pump puts out way too much pressure and regulating it down to the necessary pressure for the carb. burns the pump out.  The 4 cyl pump is the one to use.  We were going to use the mechanical Chevy pump but decided to try the Fiero pump until it gives us trouble.  There's plenty of room with for the mechanical pump if we need it.  The 4 cyl fuel pump supplies plenty 10+ lbs. of fuel pressure.  We knocked it down to 6 lbs for the carbed V8 which is all it needs, the engine runs like a top.  Now we just need to figure out what's going on with the transmission and I'm on the road.||||reg||6
Z||000005||pollock||11-08-2000||03:13 PM||||Indy was on the right track... <P>the 4 cyl TBI pump will supply too much pressure for a carb motor, good that you have regulated it.  Once you get it on the road, you may soon find out that your v8 wants more volume than the little pump can supply.  ||||reg||1
